  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM EST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nBroad isentropic ascent is ongoing this afternoon with a few pockets \nof light rain/sprinkles across northwest/central Indiana with the \nmore notable area of isentropic forcing working north from central \nIndiana. As was the case yesterday subcloud layer remains relatively \ndry with stronger moisture transport over the past 24 hours \ndisplaced well to the west. Will likely maintain some slight chance \nPoPs as this area of stronger advective forcing lifts northward into \nthis evening. This light precip may also be aided by a weak, \nslow moving mid/upper level short wave trough evident on water \nvapor imagery this afternoon across eastern Iowa/northwest \nIllinois. This initial short wave is expected to exit northwest\nOhio toward midday Monday which should allow for slight veering\nof flow and some weak speed convergence at nose of stronger low\nlevel flow Monday into Monday evening. This should provide more\nfavorable low level flow trajectories for better low level \nmoisture transport across Mid MS Rvr valley to shift east. A few\nuncertainties remain regarding precip chances for Monday \nincluding the strength of secondary upstream wave emanating from\nlarger scale western CONUS negative upper height anomaly, and \nalso mid level moisture profiles may be scoured out a bit late \ntonight into early Monday with the passage of the initial wave \nwith possibility of a drizzly precip type late tonight into \nMonday AM. With uncertainty into how moisture depths will play \ninto afternoon/evening rain chances, will cap higher model \nblended PoPs at 20-30% for now. Highs in mid 60s Monday may be a\nbit optimistic for some areas given expected shallow mixing \nheights/cloud cover.\n\nFor Monday night into Tuesday will be monitoring evolution of larger \nscale upper trough across the Four Corners region. All indications \nstill suggest most of cyclogenesis occuring in lee of Rockies before \nsystem becomes more vertically stacked late Tuesday afternoon. \nLikely/categorical PoPs have been maintained for Tuesday night as \nprimary low jet/low level moisture convergence axes shift eastward \ndownstream of Central Plains upper low. Otherwise, large scale upper \ndynamics are not overly impressive with this rapidly occluding \nsystem with larger scale low level height falls induced by upper \nforcing largely remaining southwest of the local area. This \nevolution and fairly rapid eastward spread of low level jet\naxis/mid level dry slot should keep rainfall amounts on the \nlighter side locally (quarter of an inch or less) for Tuesday \nnight into Wednesday AM. Strengthening low level height gradient\nshould provide breezy/windy conditions with this system, \nalthough core of low level jet axis not arriving until Tuesday \nnight may mitigate these winds somewhat. The main story with \nthis system will be the much above normal temperatures and a \npotential of some locations approaching record highs for Tuesday\nand record warm low temps for the date.\n\nTemps trend cooler Wed into Thu behind the occluded front with some \nscattered showers possible Thursday as moisture depths due to \nsome wrap around moisture. Even as low level thermal trough \nsettles firmly across the area by Friday, highs should still be \nnear normal for the date (highs in mid 50s). Medium range \nguidance spread in deterministic/ensemble solutions increases \nby this weekend with overall consensus in eastern Pacific jet \ncausing flow amplification once again with another development \nof an upper low expected. Large scale baroclinicity should be \nweak, so this pattern should favor quick transition to occluded \nsystems with no significant precip chances evident at this time.",
